course: biology
lesson: 398
title: Poo, wee and smells: rabbits that eat their droppings, dung beetles and the smelliest animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your pet rabbit bends down and eats something straight from its own bottom. Gross? Not for a rabbit. It is a clever trick that gets twice as much goodness out of tough grass.
explain: Poo and wee are not just waste. A dung beetle rolls dung balls to feed its young, steering by the Milky Way. Whales poo near the surface and feed the ocean. Dogs leave wee on lamp posts as messages for other dogs.
explain: Smells are messages too. A skunk's spray tells a fox «never again», a cat buries its poo to hide its smell, and a hoopoe chick squirts smelly poo at intruders.
explain: Scientists love droppings because they tell stories: which animals live in a wood, what they ate, and even how many penguins live on the ice, seen from space.
fact: Rabbits make two kinds of droppings, hard round ones and soft sticky ones, which they eat straight away so the food passes through the gut twice and more goodness is taken out of tough grass.
fact: A healthy rabbit makes hundreds of hard droppings a day.
fact: Dung beetles roll balls of dung away from the pile to eat and to feed their young, and scientists found that some use the Milky Way and the Moon to keep a straight line at night; they went straight even under a starry sky in a planetarium, a dome-shaped room that shows the night sky on its ceiling.
fact: Birds do not make watery wee; the white part of their droppings is their waste water turned into a paste, which saves water and weight for flying.
fact: Bird droppings piled up for centuries on islands were once mined as a rich fertiliser.
fact: Huge brown or pink stains of penguin droppings can be seen on satellite photos of the ice, and scientists use them to find and count penguin colonies; the colour comes from the krill and fish penguins eat.
fact: Giant pandas eat bamboo for many hours a day but digest only a small part of it, because their guts are short and made for meat, so they poo dozens of times a day and their droppings smell of fresh bamboo.
fact: Whales eat deep down and poo near the surface, bringing iron and other goodness up to tiny ocean plants that feed krill and fish and make much of the oxygen we breathe; scientists call this the whale pump.
fact: Many animals use smelly sprays or oils to scare enemies, like the skunk and its African cousin the zorilla, and hoopoe chicks squirt smelly poo and a nasty oil at intruders, so predators remember never to try again.
fact: Dogs leave wee on posts and trees as messages telling other dogs who passed and when, so a walk gives a dog its news of the day.
fact: Cats bury their droppings to hide their smell from bigger animals and rivals, and a cat that stops burying may be stressed or unwell.
fact: Rangers and scientists study droppings, which hold bits of food, hair and even DNA, to learn which animals live in a place and what they eat; rabbit droppings are little round balls, deer droppings look like dark beans and fox droppings often hold fur and seeds.
exercise: What two kinds of droppings does a rabbit make? | Hard round ones and soft sticky ones | Green ones in summer and brown ones in winter | Long thin ones and wide flat ones too | Only one kind, small, hard and round
exercise: Why does a rabbit eat its soft droppings? | So the food goes through the gut twice and more goodness is taken out | Because it is always starving and cannot find enough grass in the field to fill it up | To clean its cage | Because it cannot find grass
exercise: About how many hard droppings does a healthy rabbit make a day? | Hundreds | Just one a day | Exactly ten | A million
exercise: Why does a dung beetle roll dung balls? | To eat and to feed its young | To build a house for the winter | To play games with other beetles | To clean the field for farmers
exercise: What do some dung beetles use to keep a straight line at night? | The Milky Way and the Moon | The sweet smell of the flowers in the field | The sound of rivers and streams | Only the street lamps in town
exercise: Where did scientists test the dung beetles' starlight steering? | In a planetarium | In a swimming pool | On the Moon itself | In a deep dark cave
exercise: What is the white part of bird droppings? | Their waste water turned into a paste | Little bits of eggshell that the bird swallowed | Milk from their young | Chalk they swallowed
exercise: Why does not carrying watery wee help birds? | It saves water and weight for flying | It keeps their feathers clean and white | It makes them sing much louder and better | It helps them see much better at night
exercise: How do scientists use penguin droppings seen from space? | To find and count penguin colonies | To measure the temperature of the ice | To feed seals | To guess the weather
exercise: Why does a panda poo dozens of times a day? | It digests only a small part of the bamboo it eats | It drinks far too much water from the mountain streams | It eats only meat | Its gut is very long
exercise: What is the whale pump? | Whales pooing near the surface and feeding tiny ocean plants | Whales spraying water high into the air from their blowholes | Whales pumping water out of boats | Whales swallowing the sea
exercise: What do the tiny ocean plants fed by whale poo give us? | Much of the oxygen we breathe | All of the water that we drink | Most of our salt | Sand for the beaches
exercise: Why does a skunk spray a terrible smell? | So predators remember never to try again | To mark its favourite trees in the woods | To attract a mate from far away | To clean its fur
exercise: What do hoopoe chicks squirt at intruders? | Smelly poo and a nasty oil | Very hot water | Sticky honey from flowers | Sharp little feathers
exercise: Why does a dog sniff and wee on lamp posts? | To read and leave smell messages for other dogs | To wash the post clean for the next dog | To mark the way home only | Because it is thirsty
exercise: Why do cats bury their poo? | To hide their smell from bigger animals and rivals | To keep it safe so they can eat it later | To help plants grow | To keep the poo warm
exercise: Which droppings look like little round balls? | Rabbit droppings | Fox droppings | Bird droppings | Panda droppings
test: «my rabbit's eating his own poo, he's broken, take him to the vet» What is the best reply? | It is normal: rabbits eat their soft droppings to get more goodness from grass | Yes, healthy rabbits never do that, so he must be very ill and needs the vet | He is just very hungry, feed him chocolate | He is cleaning his cage for you
test: What would happen if a rabbit could not eat its soft droppings? | It would get less goodness out of its food | It would grow faster and bigger than ever before | Nothing at all would change | It would stop making droppings
test: In a cartoon, a beetle rolls a dung ball round in circles and gets dizzy. What does a real dung beetle try to do? | Roll it in a straight line away from the pile | Roll it round in circles to make the ball rounder | Roll it back to the pile | Roll it up the trees
test: «bird pooped on dad's car again, why's it white?» What is the right answer? | The white part is the bird's waste water made into a paste | Birds eat lots of chalk and little stones, and that comes out white | Birds drink milk | Birds paint their droppings white
test: Count them: a ranger shows pictures of round balls, dark beans and droppings with fur and seeds. How many different animals, and which? | 3: rabbit, deer and fox | 2: rabbit and fox | 3: rabbit, panda and whale | 1: a deer
test: What might happen to fish if there were far fewer whales? | There might be fewer fish, because whale poo feeds the tiny plants at the bottom of the food chain | There would be many more fish, because whales eat almost all of the fish in the sea every year | Nothing, whales have no effect | Fish would have to eat whale poo themselves
test: Why is studying droppings easier than finding shy animals themselves? | Droppings stay put and hold food, hair and even DNA | Shy animals do not really have bodies that can be found | Droppings can tell you about the animal by talking | Shy animals never poo
test: «my cat stopped burying her poo in the tray, so what» Why should the owner notice? | The cat may be stressed or unwell | It means the cat is very happy at home | Cats never bother to bury poo anyway | It means the litter is far too clean
test: «why's he sniff every lamp post on the walk, it's well boring» What is the dog doing? | Reading smell messages about which dogs passed and when | Looking for lost bones | Checking for rain | Counting the posts
test: In a cartoon, a skunk sprays a fox, and next week the fox chases it again. What would a real fox probably do? | Remember the smell and leave skunks alone | Chase skunks even more, to pay them back for the smell | Spray the skunk back | Ask the skunk for a truce
test: Count them: a panda poos 40 times a day. How many times in 2 days? | 80 | 42 | 60 | 400
test: Why does a panda that digests little need to eat so much bamboo? | It gets only a small part of the goodness, so it needs a lot | Bamboo is the richest food on Earth | It saves food for winter | It likes the taste of leaves
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the domestic rabbit consumes its soft droppings. | The food is thereby passed through the gut a second time, permitting a greater quantity of nourishment to be obtained | The rabbit is invariably deprived of food and is compelled by hunger to consume whatever material lies within its enclosure | The rabbit seeks to clean its enclosure | The droppings contain sugar which the rabbit prefers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the navigation of certain dung beetles at night? | They maintain a straight course by reference to the Milky Way and the Moon | They navigate exclusively by the scent of flowers | They navigate by the sound of flowing water | They are incapable of maintaining a straight course
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the phenomenon known as the whale pump. | Whales feed at depth and defecate near the surface, supplying nutrients to minute marine plants | Whales expel water through their blowholes in order to irrigate the ocean and to cool its surface waters | Whales pump water out of vessels at sea | Whales consume the minute plants upon which krill depend
exam: It is given that a healthy rabbit produces several hundred hard droppings daily. Ascertain the purpose served by its soft droppings. | They are consumed in order to extract further nourishment from the food | They are discarded as being of no use | They are employed to mark the boundaries of territory | They are fed to the young exclusively
